## Proof

**Euler characteristic of $\mathbb{CP}^2$.** The Betti numbers of $\mathbb{CP}^2$ are $b_0=b_2=b_4=1$ and $b_1=b_3=0$, so
$$\chi(\mathbb{CP}^2)=1-0+1-0+1=3.$$

**Effect of a single blow-up on the Euler characteristic.** Let $\pi:\widetilde{X}\to X$ be the blow-up of a smooth complex surface $X$ at a point $p$, with exceptional divisor $E\cong\mathbb{CP}^1$. Topologically, $\widetilde{X}$ is obtained from $X$ by removing a small open ball $B$ around $p$ (with $\partial B\cong S^3$) and gluing in the disk bundle $D(E)$ of the tautological line bundle over $E\cong\mathbb{CP}^1$, whose boundary is also $S^3$ (the Hopf fibration). Hence
$$\widetilde{X}=(X\setminus B^\circ)\cup_{S^3} D(E).$$
By additivity of the Euler characteristic,
$$\chi(\widetilde{X})=\chi(X\setminus B^\circ)+\chi(D(E))-\chi(S^3).$$
Now:
- $\chi(X\setminus B^\circ)=\chi(X)-\chi(B^\circ)=\chi(X)-1$, since $B^\circ$ is an open 4-ball (contractible, $\chi=1$).
- $D(E)$ deformation retracts onto $E\cong\mathbb{CP}^1$, so $\chi(D(E))=\chi(\mathbb{CP}^1)=2$.
- $\chi(S^3)=0$ (odd-dimensional sphere).

Therefore
$$\chi(\widetilde{X})=(\chi(X)-1)+2-0=\chi(X)+1.$$

Each blow-up of a point on a smooth surface increases the Euler characteristic by exactly $1$.

**Iterating $n$ blow-ups.** Blowing up $\mathbb{CP}^2$ at $n$ points (performed successively, each on the resulting smooth surface) applies this operation $n$ times. Since each blow-up increases $\chi$ by $1$,
$$\chi\bigl(\mathrm{Bl}_{n\text{ pts}}\,\mathbb{CP}^2\bigr)=\chi(\mathbb{CP}^2)+n=3+n.$$

**Result.**
$$\boxed{\chi\bigl(\mathrm{Bl}_{n\text{ pts}}\,\mathbb{CP}^2\bigr)=n+3.}$$

### PROOF COMPLETE
